1
00:00:01,110 --> 00:00:03,950
This is a timeline we built
of the Great Depression,

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,730
from its sources: the boom
of the twenties, the Crash,

3
00:00:07,780 --> 00:00:12,550
the slump in America and around the
world, and the war that ended it.

4
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:16,180
A hundred and twenty-one
moments, thirty-five durations,

5
00:00:16,230 --> 00:00:19,540
forty links, and each one
names where it comes from.

6
00:00:21,290 --> 00:00:25,490
The bands are durations. The
Great Depression itself,

7
00:00:25,540 --> 00:00:31,580
dated here from the business-cycle
peak of August 1929 to Pearl Harbor.

8
00:00:31,630 --> 00:00:35,430
Under it, the business cycle
as economists date it:

9
00:00:35,480 --> 00:00:38,830
forty-three months of contraction,
four years of recovery,

10
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:42,128
then a second recession in 1937.

11
00:00:43,728 --> 00:00:50,208
Zoom into the autumn of 1929. The
Dow peaks on September the third.

12
00:00:50,258 --> 00:00:55,518
In mid-October a Yale economist
sees a permanently high plateau.

13
00:00:55,568 --> 00:00:58,358
Then the week of the Crash,
and inside it one day,

14
00:00:58,408 --> 00:01:01,778
Black Thursday, down to the
hour: the opening bell,

15
00:01:01,828 --> 00:01:06,138
the bankers meeting at noon, and at
half past one their man on the floor,

16
00:01:06,188 --> 00:01:14,758
bidding for U.S. Steel. It held for a
day. On Monday and Tuesday the Dow lost

17
00:01:14,808 --> 00:01:16,604
almost a quarter.

18
00:01:18,234 --> 00:01:23,244
And the Crash was only the start. That
band, the Dow from peak to bottom,

19
00:01:23,294 --> 00:01:29,369
runs two years and ten months, to
July 1932: down eighty-nine percent.

20
00:01:31,099 --> 00:01:35,289
Pull out one thread:
banking. Separate by tags,

21
00:01:35,339 --> 00:01:40,729
and the panics line up as bands: Nashville
and the Bank of United States in

22
00:01:40,779 --> 00:01:45,509
1930, Chicago in 1931, the
runs after Britain left gold,

23
00:01:45,559 --> 00:01:50,812
and the winter of 1933, when state
after state closed its banks.

24
00:01:52,542 --> 00:01:56,492
Choose world instead,
and the slump is global.

25
00:01:56,542 --> 00:02:02,492
Austria's largest bank fails in May
1931, then Germany's banks close.

26
00:02:02,542 --> 00:02:06,442
Britain leaves gold that
September, Japan in December,

27
00:02:06,492 --> 00:02:12,382
and Japan recovers among the first. In
Germany six million are out of work,

28
00:02:12,432 --> 00:02:16,613
and in January 1933 Hitler
becomes chancellor.

29
00:02:18,163 --> 00:02:22,643
Open a moment to read it.
The Emergency Banking Act,

30
00:02:22,693 --> 00:02:26,983
passed in a single day, five days
after Roosevelt took office:

31
00:02:27,033 --> 00:02:31,233
what it did, where that comes
from, and what it's linked to.

32
00:02:31,283 --> 00:02:35,903
Follow a link to the first
fireside chat, three days later.

33
00:02:35,953 --> 00:02:39,074
When the banks reopened,
the deposits came back.

34
00:02:40,714 --> 00:02:45,424
The Hundred Days are a band of their
own: the Civilian Conservation Corps,

35
00:02:45,474 --> 00:02:49,164
farm relief, the Tennessee
Valley Authority, the NRA,

36
00:02:49,214 --> 00:02:51,174
and deposit insurance.

37
00:02:52,864 --> 00:02:56,524
Then the Second New Deal:
the WPA, the Wagner Act,

38
00:02:56,574 --> 00:03:00,694
Social Security. The Court
strikes down the NRA,

39
00:03:00,744 --> 00:03:07,534
and Roosevelt's plan to enlarge it
fails. In 1937 the recovery breaks:

40
00:03:07,584 --> 00:03:11,743
unemployment, fourteen percent
that year, is nineteen the next.

41
00:03:13,373 --> 00:03:17,783
One more thread: the farm.
Prices fell in 1920,

42
00:03:17,833 --> 00:03:23,823
nine years before the Crash.
Then drought, from 1930 to 1939:

43
00:03:23,873 --> 00:03:29,573
the Dust Bowl, named after
Black Sunday in April 1935.

44
00:03:29,623 --> 00:03:32,913
A year later Dorothea Lange
photographs a migrant mother,

45
00:03:32,963 --> 00:03:36,013
and four years to the
day after Black Sunday,

46
00:03:36,063 --> 00:03:38,758
Steinbeck publishes The Grapes of Wrath.

47
00:03:40,488 --> 00:03:43,008
What ended it was war: orders from Europe,

48
00:03:43,058 --> 00:03:49,308
Lend-Lease, then Pearl Harbor. One
worker in four was jobless in 1933;

49
00:03:49,358 --> 00:03:58,108
by 1942, fewer than one in twenty. And
the Dow? It didn't regain its 1929 peak

50
00:03:58,158 --> 00:04:00,306
until 1954.

51
00:04:02,036 --> 00:04:05,439
Open it, follow the links,
or build one of your own.
